/E (OpenReceive can optionally accept USDT and USDC \(also SOL and ETH\) through a swap provider you configure. The payment is swapped automatically to BTC over Lightning and settles in your wallet. The merchant never holds a stablecoin. They receive sats.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (We did this because the reality is a lot of people want to pay with this. In a lot of the world, dollar stablecoins are how people actually hold and move money. We think the best way to bring Bitcoin adoption to those people is to have the merchants actually receive Lightning. Every one of those payments ends up as a bitcoin buy, and every merchant who turns it on is running a Lightning checkout.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (If you don't want it, don't turn it on. Swaps are off unless you add a provider. One honest caveat: swaps add complexity, because a short or late deposit has to be refunded on the payer's chain. That flow is documented.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
