/E (Marriage, Family and Relationships- Marriage and dating are, in essence, a market based on partner selection and matching. Household specialization can be seen as simple division of labor, and the choice to have children often depends on the opportunity cost of lost wages. Economists will even look at how changes in household income or laws affect divorce rates, or how data from dating apps demonstrate revealed preferences.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (The list goes on and on: religion, sociology, biology, technology all have an economics angle. This has produced something of a superiority complex amongst economists. A 2005 survey of economics graduate students found 77% believe that economics is the most scientific of the social sciences. This can be because the mathematical models and empirical techniques used in economics generates more precise predictions than the tools used in political science, sociology and elsewhere, and is capable of distinguishing causal effects from mere correlation. Moreover, incentives are the root of human behavior and many of our actions can be explained by how they personally affect us.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11 343 0 R 13]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [12]
/Pg 15 0 R
/P 343 0 R
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (However, this expansion of economics into other fields, sometimes termed "Economics Imperialism," has produced a backlash. Critics state that economists oversimplify human behavior, ignore research and scholarship elsewhere, and are trying to establish economics as the dominant social science. Sociologists claim "human beings are not merely utility maximizing individuals," and that treating them as purely independent decision-makers responding to incentives is misguided. For example, marriage isn't just a matching market with individuals looking to maximize utility; there's religious norms, romantic preferences, and family obligations to consider. Likewise, political scientists will say that politics is more than rational self-interest, and healthcare experts argue patients are not just units in a cost-benefit calculation. In response to the criticism, some economists claim that other academics are threatened by economics purely out of selfish protectionism and hostility to novel theories that challenge their field's internal dogma.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (So, the question I ask of you is: should economists stay in their lane?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [15]
>>
